introduction:
  bigQuestion: "How do binary values and logic circuits become real system decisions?"
  overview: "This unit links number representation, Boolean logic, and operating-system control. You will convert between number systems, describe logic-gate behavior, construct logic diagrams, and compare process scheduling strategies."
  journey: "You will describe binary/decimal/hex representation, describe purpose and use of logic gates, construct logic diagrams from Boolean expressions, describe the operating system role, and compare FCFS, Round Robin, and Priority scheduling."

concepts:
  - title: "Data representation"
    icon: "fa-binary"
    content: |
      <p>Computers represent all values using binary patterns.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Binary:</strong> machine-level state representation.</li>
          <li><strong>Hexadecimal:</strong> compact readable form of binary.</li>
          <li><strong>Decimal:</strong> human-facing arithmetic representation.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Logic gates and diagrams"
    icon: "fa-microchip"
    content: |
      <p>Boolean logic is implemented physically through gate circuits.</p>
      <ul>
          <li>Core gates: AND, OR, NOT.</li>
          <li>Derived gates: NAND, NOR, XOR, XNOR.</li>
          <li>Construction flow: expression to diagram to verification.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Operating system role"
    icon: "fa-desktop"
    content: |
      <p>The OS coordinates hardware resources and user/application access.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>CPU/process management</strong> for concurrent tasks.</li>
          <li><strong>Memory and device management</strong> through controlled access.</li>
          <li><strong>File and interface services</strong> for usable systems.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Scheduling trade-offs"
    icon: "fa-list-check"
    content: |
      <p>Scheduling determines responsiveness and fairness under load.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>FCFS:</strong> simple queue order.</li>
          <li><strong>Round Robin:</strong> fixed time slices for fairness.</li>
          <li><strong>Priority:</strong> urgency-based service with starvation risk.</li>
      </ul>

terminology:
  - term: "Binary / Decimal / Hexadecimal"
    definition: "Base-2, base-10, and base-16 number systems used for representation and conversion."
  - term: "Logic gate"
    definition: "Hardware element implementing a Boolean function on binary inputs."
  - term: "Boolean expression"
    definition: "Logical statement combining variables with operators such as AND/OR/NOT."
  - term: "Truth table"
    definition: "Table of outputs for every possible input combination."
  - term: "Logic diagram"
    definition: "Circuit layout showing signal flow through gates."
  - term: "Operating system"
    definition: "System software managing CPU, memory, peripherals, and user interaction."
  - term: "Scheduling"
    definition: "Method used by the OS to allocate CPU time among processes."
  - term: "FCFS"
    definition: "First Come First Served non-preemptive scheduling policy."
  - term: "Round Robin"
    definition: "Preemptive policy rotating processes using a fixed time quantum."
  - term: "Priority scheduling"
    definition: "Policy selecting process order by priority level."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "Hexadecimal is a different kind of data"
    correction: "Hex is another representation of the same binary value, not a separate data type."
  - mistake: "XOR behaves like OR"
    correction: "XOR is true only when exactly one input is true."
  - mistake: "Fastest scheduler is always best"
    correction: "Scheduling choice depends on fairness, responsiveness, and workload requirements."

